An equilateral triangle with side length $6$ is divided into $4$ congruent equilateral triangles with side length $3$ by joining the midpoints of the sides. $1$ circle is inscribed inside each of the congruent equilateral triangles, and $1$ is inscribed inside the original equilateral triangle. Find the shaded region.
[asy]
size(160);
pair A1,B1,C1,A2,B2,C2;
A1=(0,0);
B1=(2,0);
C1=(1,sqrt(3));
A2=(1/3,0);
B2=(7/6,sqrt(3)/6);
C2=(5/6,sqrt(3)/2);
draw(circle(A1,B1)); draw(circle(A2,B2)); draw(circle(C1,C1)); draw(circle((3/2,sqrt(3)/2),sqrt(3)/2)); label("$2\sqrt{3}$", (3,0),S);
[/asy] <calculate> The shaded area is the difference between the area of the small triangle and the area of the small circle. The area of the small triangle is $\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} \times 3^2 = \frac{9\sqrt{3}}{4}$. The area of the small circle is $\pi \times \left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{6}\right)^2 = \frac{\pi}{12}$. Therefore, the shaded area is $\frac{9\sqrt{3}}{4} - \frac{\pi}{12}$.

